---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoRemux from '@/components/tools/media/VideoRemux.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mkv-to-mp4">
  <VideoRemux
    client:load
    from="mkv"
    target="mp4"
    hint="Matroska files of any size. A 4 GB film is fine, because nothing is uploaded"
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Turning a Matroska file into something your TV will accept</h2>
    <p>
      MKV is the most capable video container in common use: it can hold any codec, any number of audio
      tracks, chapters, attachments and several kinds of subtitle at once. That flexibility is why
      hardware refuses it. Smart TVs, games consoles, older Android phones and most upload forms support
      only MP4, and they reject the file for its wrapper alone. Usually the video inside is ordinary
      H.264 that the device could decode easily.
    </p>
    <p>
      So MKV to MP4 is a change of wrapper, and it costs no quality. FFmpeg reads the Matroska index,
      copies each compressed packet across and writes an MP4 index in front of it. No frame is decoded.
    </p>

    <h2>What survives the conversion, and what does not</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Inside the MKV</th>
            <th>In the MP4</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>H.264 or HEVC video</td>
            <td>Copied unchanged</td>
            <td>MP4 carries both natively</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AAC or MP3 audio</td>
            <td>Copied unchanged</td>
            <td>Both are standard MP4 audio</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FLAC, DTS or Vorbis audio</td>
            <td>Re-encoded to AAC 160 kbps</td>
            <td>MP4 has no slot for them</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>VP9 or AV1 video</td>
            <td>Re-encoded to H.264 (slow)</td>
            <td>Players expect these in WebM</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Second and third audio tracks</td>
            <td>Dropped. The first track is kept</td>
            <td>This tool does not offer track selection</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Soft subtitles (SRT, ASS, PGS)</td>
            <td>Dropped</td>
            <td>MP4 has no equivalent for ASS or PGS</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Chapters and attachments</td>
            <td>Dropped</td>
            <td>Not carried by the copy path</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      If you need subtitles, keep the MKV and play it in a media player that supports MKV. To burn
      subtitles into the picture is a re-encode and makes them permanent, which people rarely want.
    </p>

    <h2>Very large files</h2>
    <p>
      Here a browser tool has a real limit. The input and the output are both in the tab’s memory, and
      browsers give WebAssembly about 2 GB. The copy path needs no decoding buffer in addition, so
      multi-gigabyte films usually convert on a desktop with free RAM. On a phone, or with thirty other
      tabs open, they may not. If memory runs out, the page tells you so in a sentence.
    </p>
    <p>
      An upload converter means 4 GB up, often against a 1 GB cap on free accounts, a queue, and 4 GB
      back down. Here there is no transfer.
    </p>

    <h2>Does converting MKV to MP4 lose quality?</h2>
    <p>
      Not when the streams are copied. The output is bit-for-bit the same video data in a different
      wrapper, and the file size is within a fraction of a percent of the original. The page tells you
      before it starts if your file qualifies. Quality is lost only when something in the MKV has no
      MP4 equivalent, and then you get a clear warning and a choice.
    </p>

    <h2>Related jobs</h2>
    <p>
      For only the soundtrack, <a href="/mkv-to-mp3">MKV to MP3</a> skips the video. If the file is too
      big for a messaging app, <a href="/compress-video">compress video</a> targets a size. If it is too
      long, <a href="/trim-video">trim video</a> cuts it without re-encoding.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
